{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhAPy5Mgt74b2zahjWuA9E","type":"tool_result","content":"bugs:\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-09-27T170100\n    date: \"2026-09-27\"\n    title: \"G1 accepts a mandatory requirement's satisfied_by_reference claim without evidence, before G2/G5 are known to catch a false one\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: domain\n    what_happened: >\n      A material answer bound to a mandatory requirement passes G1 (`gateG1`, `src/domain/change/decide.ts`)\n      as soon as the requirement is named, is mandatory, and the binding is observable — G1 never reads\n      `satisfied_by_reference`. A specification report can mark a brand-new, never-implemented\n      requirement `satisfied_by_reference: true` (a claim reserved for behaviour the project already\n      honours, per the `specify` role's own instructions) and still clear G1 unchallenged.\n    what_expected: >\n      A material answer resolved only by a false `satisfied_by_reference` claim should not clear a gate\n      on the model's word alone — some control downstream must be known to catch it, or G1 itself must\n      refuse it.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Write a specification report whose mandatory requirement is marked `satisfied_by_reference: true` for behaviour the reference tree does not have, with a material answer bound to it.\"\n      - \"Advance the change through G1: `gateG1` names no reason and passes.\"\n      - \"Whether `preparation.ts`'s discrimination against the reference (feeding G2) or G5's evidence review would still catch the false claim before integration was not traced in this pass.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `satisfied_by_reference` is read by `preparation.ts` (`~line 116`) to flag a requirement as\n      `undiscriminated` when the reference tree fails to exercise it, feeding a later gate's evidence —\n      but `gateG1` (`~decide.ts:505-515`) never consults it before passing the requirements document, so\n      a false claim is not refused at the gate meant to bind an answer to a real obligation; whether it\n      is refused later depends on control paths this pass did not trace to a verdict.\n    evidence: >\n      Read during the round-1 review of e01s03 (`specs/verifications/e01s03-relecture.md`, P2):\n      `gateG1`'s mandatory-answer loop names no check on `satisfied_by_reference`; `preparation.ts:116-126`\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Name in the notes of the control each file left unread as binary, or read a file whose text holds a NUL as\n      text when its name does not mark a binary format.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-09T123350\n    date: \"2026-10-09\"\n    title: \"The survey section of the report gives the verdict of a control's measure without the notes of its limits, so a file a control left unread is seen only in the dossier\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      `surveySection` (`src/application/report.ts`) carries, for each requirement, the control and its verdict, the\n      findings and the blind spots, and drops `limits.notes` of the evidence the survey measured on. The notes reach\n      the report only through `control_limit` risks, gathered from the evidence on the current candidate. On a\n      Maven reactor whose `repository.properties` and `UserLoader.java` hold a NUL byte, the survey shows the control\n      `configured-links` as PASS while its evidence notes that both files were not read: the reader of the report\n      does not learn that two files were skipped.\n    what_expected: >\n      The survey section names, under a control's measure, the notes of the limits it ran into, as the risks do for\n      a candidate.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Adopt the map on the reactor of `test/helpers/configured-links-reactor.ts` with a NUL byte after the line of `domain/src/main/resources/repository.properties` that names `io.demo.infra.UserStore`, and ask `/495 report`: the survey section shows `configured-links` PASS and names neither file, while `evidence.document.limits.notes` of the reference evidence names both.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `surveySection` maps a survey measure to its control id and verdict only; the notes of the evidence are read\n      for the risks of the candidate, never for the survey.\n    evidence: >\n      Observed at the acceptance run of `e11s09` on dossier `e11s09-t5-nul` (notes present in SQLite, absent from the\n      report); `surveySection` reads no note on `main` either, so the defect predates the branch.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Carry the notes of the evidence each survey measure rests on into the survey section, in both languages.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-09T150000\n    date: \"2026-10-09\"\n    title: \"A verification companion entry without a string id makes \\\"undefined\\\" a known promise id, so another entry may depend on it\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: cycle\n    what_happened: >\n      `lireDeclarations` (`cycle/src/verification-contract.ts`) builds the set of known ids with `String(p.id)` over\n      every object entry, and `lirePromesse` returns `id: String(p.id)`. An entry whose `id` is absent is reported at\n      `promesses[i].id`, yet the string \"undefined\" joins the known ids: a second entry declaring\n      `\"dependances\": [\"undefined\"]` passes without an error at its own path, and the diagnostic of the first\n      promise carries the id \"undefined\" where its type says null for an undeclared promise.\n    what_expected: >\n      Only a non-empty string id is a known promise id; a dependency on anything else is located as unknown, and a\n      promise read from an entry without a valid id carries no id.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Write a companion with two entries for the two scenarios of a story, the first without `id`, the second with `\\\"dependances\\\": [\\\"undefined\\\"]`; read the story: the errors name `promesses[0].id` and nothing at `promesses[1].dependances[0]`.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The known ids and the read id are taken through `String(...)` rather than kept only when `texteNonVide`.\n    evidence: >\n      Introduced by the branch e38s01, which adds the companion reader. Read in `lireDeclarations` and\n      `lirePromesse`; the readiness of the story is already false through the\n      `promesses[0].id` error, so no promise is declared ready by this path.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Keep in the known ids only the entries whose id is a non-empty string, and refuse a dependency on any other value.\n    status: open\nregistry-fixed.yaml\nregistry.yaml","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bfbbd61b-a8c8-4070-810f-c6aa17c1e9f3","uuid":"4f59fa68-8f61-4dc8-8afd-fdbe04c5f94a","timestamp":"2026-10-09T13:23:12.806Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"bugs:\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-09-27T170100\n    date: \"2026-09-27\"\n    title: \"G1 accepts a mandatory requirement's satisfied_by_reference claim without evidence, before G2/G5 are known to catch a false one\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: domain\n    what_happened: >\n      A material answer bound to a mandatory requirement passes G1 (`gateG1`, `src/domain/change/decide.ts`)\n      as soon as the requirement is named, is mandatory, and the binding is observable — G1 never reads\n      `satisfied_by_reference`. A specification report can mark a brand-new, never-implemented\n      requirement `satisfied_by_reference: true` (a claim reserved for behaviour the project already\n      honours, per the `specify` role's own instructions) and still clear G1 unchallenged.\n    what_expected: >\n      A material answer resolved only by a false `satisfied_by_reference` claim should not clear a gate\n      on the model's word alone — some control downstream must be known to catch it, or G1 itself must\n      refuse it.\n    steps_to_rep